<ArticleLayout
  title="Early Flu Symptoms — First Warning Signs"
  description="Learn to recognize the earliest signs of influenza before full symptoms develop, including incubation period and what happens in the first 24 hours."
  publishDate="July 2026"
  lastReviewed="July 2026"
>
  <p>
    Influenza often announces itself before the classic fever, cough, and body aches take hold. Recognizing these early warning signs can help you isolate sooner, seek antiviral treatment within the critical window, and reduce transmission to others. The <strong>CDC</strong> notes that people with influenza are most contagious in the first 3–4 days after illness begins — often before they realize they are sick.
  </p>

  <div class="callout-info">
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The earliest flu symptoms often appear suddenly — unlike the gradual onset of a common cold. If you feel like you were "hit by a truck" over the course of a few hours, influenza is a strong possibility.
  </div>

  <h2>The Incubation Period: What Happens Before Symptoms</h2>
  <p>
    After exposure to the influenza virus, there is an <strong>incubation period of 1 to 4 days</strong>, with an average of about 2 days (CDC, WHO). During this time the virus replicates in the respiratory tract without causing noticeable symptoms — but you may already be contagious. Studies show that viral shedding can begin <strong>24 hours before symptom onset</strong>, meaning you can spread the flu before you know you have it.
  </p>

