  <h2>The First Warning Signs (Prodromal Phase)</h2>
  <p>
    The transition from feeling well to being unwell with influenza is often strikingly abrupt. Research published in the <em>Journal of Infectious Diseases</em> describes a <strong>prodromal phase</strong> — a brief period of non-specific symptoms that precedes full illness. Key early indicators include:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Sudden Fatigue and Malaise</h3>
  <p>
    Many people report an overwhelming sense of tiredness that seems disproportionate to their activity level. This is caused by <strong>cytokine release</strong> (particularly interleukin-6 and tumor necrosis factor-alpha) as the immune system mounts its initial response. You may feel an urge to lie down or a "heavy" sensation in your limbs.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Subtle Sore Throat or Scratchiness</h3>
  <p>
    As the virus begins infecting the epithelial cells of the upper respiratory tract, a dry, scratchy sensation in the throat is common. Unlike strep throat, this is usually mild at first and may be easily dismissed — until it intensifies over the next 12–24 hours.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Headache</h3>
  <p>
    A dull, frontal headache often appears early. This is linked to the release of prostaglandins and other inflammatory mediators triggered by the immune response to the virus. Unlike tension headaches, it may feel diffuse and unrelenting.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Chills or Feeling "Off"</h3>
  <p>
    Before a measurable fever develops, many individuals experience <strong>chills or rigors</strong> — the body's attempt to raise its core temperature to fight the virus. You might feel unusually cold even in a warm room, or experience a general sense that something is "not right."
  </p>

  <h3>5. Dry Cough</h3>
  <p>
    A non-productive cough may be among the first respiratory signs. The NHS notes that an early flu cough is typically dry and persistent, unlike the wet, productive cough that may develop later with secondary bacterial infection.
  </p>

  <h2>The First 24 Hours of Symptomatic Flu</h2>
  <p>
    Once full symptoms emerge, the first 24 hours are critical. The CDC recommends that antiviral medications such as <strong>oseltamivir (Tamiflu)</strong> or <strong>baloxavir marboxil (Xofluza)</strong> be started within <strong>48 hours of symptom onset</strong> for maximum effectiveness. Here's what to expect:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fever escalates quickly</strong> — Typically 100°F–104°F (37.8°C–40°C), often accompanied by sweating and chills alternating.</li>
    <li><strong>Muscle and joint pain intensifies</strong> — Myalgia and arthralgia become pronounced, especially in the back, arms, and legs.</li>
    <li><strong>Respiratory symptoms deepen</strong> — The cough becomes more frequent and may cause chest discomfort.</li>
    <li><strong>Appetite loss</strong> — Nausea and lack of appetite are common, especially in children.</li>
    <li><strong>Eye symptoms</strong> — Photophobia (light sensitivity) and eye pain on movement may occur.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-warning">
    <strong>Antiviral Window:</strong> Antiviral medications are most effective when started within 48 hours of symptom onset. If you are at high risk for complications — including adults 65+, young children, pregnant people, or those with chronic conditions — contact your healthcare provider as soon as you notice early symptoms.
  </div>

  <h2>Early Flu vs. Early Cold: Key Differences</h2>
