  <h2>What to Do in the First 24 Hours</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Isolate immediately.</strong> Stay home from work or school. The CDC recommends staying home for at least 24 hours after fever resolves without fever-reducing medication.</li>
    <li><strong>Contact your healthcare provider.</strong> If you are in a high-risk group or symptoms are severe, ask about antiviral medication.</li>
    <li><strong>Rest aggressively.</strong> Your body needs energy to fight the virus. Pushing through early fatigue can prolong recovery.</li>
    <li><strong>Hydrate.</strong> Fever increases fluid loss. Water, herbal tea, and electrolyte solutions are best; avoid alcohol and caffeine.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor symptoms.</strong> Track your temperature and note any worsening of respiratory symptoms.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout-warning">
    <strong>When to Seek Immediate Care:</strong> If you experience difficulty breathing, persistent chest pain or pressure, confusion, severe muscle pain (unable to walk), or bluish lips or face, seek emergency medical attention immediately. These are CDC-designated emergency warning signs.
  </div>

